  "result": "Fixed. The quote now prints **27.06**.\n\n**Root cause:** the bug was in `to_cents` in `shop/money.py`. It padded the decimal part with a zero on the left instead of the right, so `\"12.5\"` was read as 12.05 instead of 12.50. That gave 1205 \u00d7 2 = 2410 cents plus 199 cents tax, which printed 26.09. It only went wrong for prices with one decimal place.\n\n**Fix:** I changed `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")` to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`. Now `\"12.5\"` becomes 1250 cents, so 2500 plus 206 cents tax (8.25%) = 2706, which prints 27.06.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran the suite with `python3 -m unittest`. All 6 tests pass. None of them test `to_cents` directly, which is why this slipped through. I can add a regression test for one-decimal prices if you'd like.",
